'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01e449d2-66b1-51ce-ab1c-c5b5cc5bbcf1', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01e449d2-66b1-51ce-ab1c-c5b5cc5bbcf1'}, {'abstract': 'Hospitals are on the brink of collapse, cemeteries are burying people in mass graves, and still, we refuse to take this virus seriously.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/08/opinion/brazil-coronavirus-bolsonaro.html', 'snippet': 'Hospitals are on the brink of collapse, cemeteries are burying people in mass graves, and still, we refuse to take this virus seriously.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SÃO PAULO, Brazil — It’s been almost three months since my toddler left the apartment. We’ve been enduring as best as we can: We spend countless afternoons at the balcony watching the street and counting red cars; we open and close all the curtains; we pile up boxes of paper tissues and make mountains; we invent stories about our neighbors based on the smells of their cooking. Recently, she has started to play with her own shadow. This was a wise move, since both of her parents are exhausted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/opinion/08barbara1/merlin_172937109_e39dc9ba-53ce-4333-b75c-a5502be93107-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/08/opinion/08barbara1/merlin_172937109_e39dc9ba-53ce-4333-b75c-a5502be93107-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/opinion/08barbara1/merlin_172937109_e39dc9ba-53ce-4333-b75c-a5502be93107-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N