iddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/024e9e7c-a18f-5d6f-bd55-293fa6f207cb', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/024e9e7c-a18f-5d6f-bd55-293fa6f207cb'}, {'abstract': 'Afghans once tuned into Radio Afghanistan twice a day to hear the reading of death notices. But in an age of social media, the voice of the nation has lost much of its sway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/08/world/asia/for-decades-this-radio-station-named-the-dead-few-still-listen.html', 'snippet': 'Afghans once tuned into Radio Afghanistan twice a day to hear the reading of death notices. But in an age of social media, the voice of the nation has lost much of its sway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Through decades of coups, invasions and endless war, Afghans tuned their radios to Radio Afghanistan every morning at 7 and every afternoon at 4:05 to hear the names of the newly dead.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/09/world/08afghan-radio-dispatch-A1-refer/merlin_172206279_0cfb9636-f86e-4ae9-b5f9-520dc6541faf-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/09/world/08afghan-radio-dispatch-A1-refer/merlin_172206279_0cfb9636-f86e-4ae9-b5f9-520dc6541faf-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/09/world/08afghan-radio-dispatch-A1-refer/merlin_172206279_0cfb9636-f86e-4ae9-b5f9-520dc6541faf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credi