'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-opinionvideo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-09T08:50:03+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wanda Cooper-Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper-Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2e45b0bf-7ebe-56d2-b8bc-86d463b24713', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2e45b0bf-7ebe-56d2-b8bc-86d463b24713'}, {'abstract': 'Cities from New York to Los Angeles consider changes to police funding, and others are banning chokeholds. Mr. Floyd’s brother will testify at a congressional hearing this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/09/us/george-floyd-funeral-protests.html', 'snippet': 'Cities from New York to Los Angeles consider changes to police funding, and others are banning chokeholds. Mr. Floyd’s brother will testify at a congressional hearing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than two weeks after George Floyd gasped under the knee of a Minneapolis police officer that he could not breathe — an embodiment of the suffocation long felt by black Americans — the mayor of Houston stood at his funeral on Tuesday and announced that he would ban the city’s police from using chokeholds or strangleholds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharpton Delivers Eulogy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'George Floyd Protests (2020)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-09T08:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/448b7495-d23c-5858-b4c5-76e03126bec7', 'word_count': 4111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/448b7495-d23c-5858-b4c5-76e03126bec7'}, {'abstract': 'Thin paper allows the paste to seep through. Amplify the work of artists from the neighborhood where you’re postering.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/09/magazine/how-to-wheat-paste-posters.html', 'snippet': 'Thin paper allows the paste to seep through. Amplify the work of artists from the neighborhood where you’re postering.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wheat paste is a way to get art and messaging into public spaces,” says Mark Strandquist, an artist from Philadelphia, who was walking around his neighborhood in early March, past storefronts covered over in plywood, when he realized those boards and empty windows could serve as canvases. He put out a call to artists, and within a few weeks the project, called Fill the Walls with Hope, had collected dozens of downloadable images from them. Strandquist and his fellow artists have put up more than 1,000 posters with a home-cooked glue concoction made from flour and water. Many of the posters share messages of public health and safety, among them, “Mask Up” and “Don’t Let Racism Go Viral.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14Mag-Tip-01/14Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 911, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14Mag-Tip-01/14Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 911}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14Mag-Tip-01/14Mag-Tip-01-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazi