ovement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '#MeToo Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-10T09:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Farhad Manjoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Farhad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manjoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d783527-850d-5c63-8555-613756c86722', 'word_count': 1068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d783527-850d-5c63-8555-613756c86722'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of the killing of George Floyd, there has been a leftward turn. Will it stick?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/10/opinion/george-floyd-protests-trump.html', 'snippet': 'In the wake of the killing of George Floyd, there has been a leftward turn. Will it stick?', 'lead_paragraph': 'America is at a racial and political crossroads. Protests over the past two weeks in response to an interrelated set of issues and events — the killing of George Floyd, police brutality, the Covid pandemic, a nation in lockdown, joblessness, a devastated economy and a presidential election — give rise to a key question. Will the Democratic coalition of minorities and liberal whites emerge empowered?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/opinion/10edsall1/merlin_173293608_a89e4607-06d5-4d10-aa6a-532f29597d4c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/10/opinion/10edsall1/merlin_173293608_a89e4607-06d5-4d10-aa6a-532f29597d4c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth