dline': {'main': 'A Library of Diversion While You Figure Things Out', 'kicker': 'At Home Newsletter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-10T17:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'AtHome', 'section_name': 'At Home', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/aced91ec-3efe-5e45-a908-65037e8ed1a7', 'word_count': 702, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aced91ec-3efe-5e45-a908-65037e8ed1a7'}, {'abstract': 'In the late 1960s, Fela decided that he had no use for love songs or party tunes; he wanted to challenge unjust authority.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/10/arts/music/fela-kuti-afrobeat-playlist.html', 'snippet': 'In the late 1960s, Fela decided that he had no use for love songs or party tunes; he wanted to challenge unjust authority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Resistance is etched into Afrobeat, the music forged by the Nigerian bandleader and activist Fela Kuti in the 1970s and 1980s. It’s in lyrics that denounce brutality, corruption, apathy, fear and the legacy of colonialism. And it’s in songs that deliberately stretch out, seizing time itself with African-rooted funk grooves that don’t rush but don’t ease off either, merging dance propulsion with unmistakable tension and determination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/arts/10list-fela/merlin_173399211_2f57b11d-3da1-460d-bac4-3f4ac0fa2a23-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/10/a