e': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-10T19:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Parenting', 'section_name': 'Parenting', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Gale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cd5aaab-133f-5c4f-8e75-4f85bb9ab958', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cd5aaab-133f-5c4f-8e75-4f85bb9ab958'}, {'abstract': 'Conversations on the platform stretch our understanding of the politics of the possible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/10/opinion/sunday/twitter-protest-politics.html', 'snippet': 'Conversations on the platform stretch our understanding of the politics of the possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February, I declared, somewhat winkingly, that Twitter is real life. My argument was not that what happens on that social media website is broadly representative of popular opinion but that what happens on Twitter is a good barometer of enthusiasm around movement-building and fandoms. And that elites tend to undervalue or dismiss what happens on the platform, suggesting that those loud voices making them uncomfortable aren’t accurate indicators of lived experiences.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/merlin_173184537_5f74b0d0-a490-41b9-a29b-be6999b116bc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/merlin_173184537_5f74b0d0-a490-41b9-a29b-be6999b116bc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/merlin_173184537_5f74b0d0-a490-41b9-a29b-be6999b116bc-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/merlin_173184537_5f74b0d0-a490-41b9-a29b-be6999b116bc-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/10warzel1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/10warzel1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/opinion/10warzel1/10warzel1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Floyd Protests Show That Twitter Is Real Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Protests Show That Twitter Is Real Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'George Floyd Protests (2020)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black Lives Matter Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-10T19:11:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Warzel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warzel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ce00843-ff77-516c-a0b2-0201483d7b2e', 'word_count': 1450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ce00843-ff77-516c-a0b2-0201483d7b2e'}, {'abstract': 'Some are reconsidering the rules surrounding protests and demonstrations on and off the field.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/10/sports/national-anthem-policies.html', 'snippet': 'Some are reconsidering the rules surrounding protests and demonstrations on and off the field.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Colin Kaepernick and other athletes caused a stir in 2016 by kneeling for the national anthem to protest racism and police brutality, leagues scrambled to set policies on such demonstrations. Now, in the wake of the killing of George Floyd and the nationwide protests it inspired, the issue may well arise again as American sports return. And some leagues are revisiting their rules.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/sports/10anthem-1/10anthem-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/10/sports/10anthem-1/10anthem-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/sports/10anthem-1/10anthem-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image