, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 5}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aef5ff49-b5ce-582c-a12f-5b9431909797', 'word_count': 3051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aef5ff49-b5ce-582c-a12f-5b9431909797'}, {'abstract': 'He has nothing but contempt for the institutions that exist to keep presidents in check.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/11/opinion/trump-lies.html', 'snippet': 'He has nothing but contempt for the institutions that exist to keep presidents in check.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lies and obfuscations pile up. No, it wasn’t tear gas used to clear Lafayette Park for President Trump’s Bible-waving photo-op last Monday night, Attorney General William Barr said on CBS’s “Face the Nation” on Sunday. Rather it was “pepper balls,” he said. “Pepper spray is not a chemical irritant. It’s not chemical.” Wrong, according to The Washington Post; pepper balls are very much a chemical irritant. The paper awarded the nation’s top law enforcement officer four Pinocchios for his claim.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/opinion/11Tomasky/11Tomasky-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Does Trump Lie?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Does Trump Lie?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2020', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-11T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Tomasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tomasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/af3cae16-8822-5237-bb8f-90ce6405fb12', 'word_count': 1023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af3cae16-8822-5237-bb8f-90ce6405fb12'}, {'abstract': 'A poem that asks our most present questions: What comes next? Who knows what?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/11/magazine/poem-lion.html', 'snippet': 'A poem that asks our most present questions: What comes next? Who knows what?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having met lions only from a distance in zoos, I did not consider that in the wild they can carry the scent of the dead prey they have recently consumed, and thereby stink. Poems can give us new information. The idea that something haunting and dangerous strolls through the camp at night, though, feels like recent days — tented up against the virus, we sleep our fitful sleeps. Patricia Kirkpatrick’s splendid new book, “Blood Moon,” carries us away to so many places and helps us “see farther” indeed, even asking our most present questions. What comes next? Who knows what? Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 553}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/14/magazine/14mag-poem-02/14mag-poem-02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Lion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-11T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Kirkpatrick and Naomi Shihab Nye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirkpatrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Naomi', 'middlename': 'Shihab', 'lastname': 'Nye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81713a17-64ed-5731-950f-93993c05c2c1', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81713a17-64ed-5731-950f-93993c05c2c1'}, {'abstract': 'My father the doctor maintained that hand-washing was our family’s single greatest obligation to one another and to the world. Is there any hope we’ll all keep doing it?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/11/well/live/the-hand-washing-wars.html', 'snippet': 'My father the doctor maintained that hand-washing was our family’s single greatest obligation to one another and to the world. Is there any hope we’ll all keep doing it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'My neurologist father maintained that hand-washing was our family’s single greatest obligation to one another and to the world. “As you know, it’s the No. 1 way to prevent disease transmission,” Dr. Beauregard Lee Bercaw would say to my brother and me every single night before sitting down to dinner. “Your hands are clean, right?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/well/bercaw-photo/bercaw-photo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 317, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/well/bercaw-photo/bercaw-photo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 317, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/well/bercaw-photo/bercaw-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/08/well/bercaw-photo/bercaw-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/08/well/bercaw-photo/bercaw-photo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hand-Washing Wars', 'kicker': 'Voices', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathrooms and Toilets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus Reopenings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-11T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Live', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Stearns Bercaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': 'Stearns', 'lastname': 'Bercaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acf6ce9a-96b6-5c7f-a483-649856dde319', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acf6ce9a-96b6-5c7f-a483-649856dde319'}, {'abstract': 'The PGA Tour’s first event in three months will be contested without fans, fist bumps or Tiger Woods. But even with constraints, golfers are eager to play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/11/sports/golf/pga-tour-charles-schwab.html', 'snippet': 'The PGA Tour’s first event in three months will be contested without fans, fist bumps or Tiger Woods. But even with constraints, golfers are eager to play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The PGA Tour returns from a 90-day layoff on Thursday with a tournament in Fort Worth that will be contested without spectators and with one new golfing ritual: Players are directed to sanitize their hands after every hole while their caddies wipe down golf bags with disinfectant.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/10/sports/10golf-advance-2/merlin_173398494_d05f2964-a2bf-4673-9021-059ecd47c1d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/10/sports/10golf-advance-2/merlin_173398494_d05f2964-a2bf-4673-9021-059ecd47c1d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20