nt': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7591b19-bd6e-571f-afad-e5c0fa5321a9'}, {'abstract': 'Set in a downscale New Jersey Jewish country club in the mid-1980s, the Amazon series has a laid-back, low-stakes vibe that fans of “Dazed and Confused” should appreciate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/12/arts/television/comfort-viewing-3-reasons-i-love-red-oaks.html', 'snippet': 'Set in a downscale New Jersey Jewish country club in the mid-1980s, the Amazon series has a laid-back, low-stakes vibe that fans of “Dazed and Confused” should appreciate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Within a few years after Richard Linklater’s “Dazed and Confused” tanked in theaters in 1993, cheap VHS copies were a staple of dorm rooms and off-campus housing, like shakily constructed loft beds and cereal for dinner. During that period, I was wrapping up my senior year and entering a blurry, two-year townie purgatory in Athens, Ga., before graduate school, which made me the target audience for this particular cult phenomenon. I’ve seen “Dazed and Confused” several times, but in a way I’ve seen it dozens of times because those VCRs were whirring nonstop, on the periphery of parties or other after-hours shenanigans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/arts/12comfort-redoaks1/merlin_86649493_c6230517-2b23-4ada-b9eb-b6bdfd5c5843-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/12/arts/12comfort-redoaks1/merlin_86649493_c6230517-2b23-4ada-b9eb-b6bdfd5c5843-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/12/arts/12comfort-redoaks1/merlin_86649493_c6230517-2b23-4ada-b9eb-b6bdfd5c5843-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, 