ame': 'Oliver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a634a791-8053-5eab-a4c5-7daae1759101', 'word_count': 1450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a634a791-8053-5eab-a4c5-7daae1759101'}, {'abstract': 'In Candace Bushnell and Katie Cotugno’s “Rules for Being a Girl,” a high school senior is delighted to be alone with a teacher she has a crush on — until he kisses her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/12/books/review/candace-bushnell-katie-cotugno-rules-for-being-a-girl.html', 'snippet': 'In Candace Bushnell and Katie Cotugno’s “Rules for Being a Girl,” a high school senior is delighted to be alone with a teacher she has a crush on — until he kisses her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a grim familiarity to stories about young girls falling prey to men in positions of power. As readers, we recognize the girl’s first heady thrill at being called special by someone who seems to have the authority above all others to declare it, and then we follow that plotline to its heart-rending conclusion. The best-selling authors Candace Bushnell and Katie Cotugno explore this territory in RULES FOR BEING A GIRL (Balzer + Bray, 304 pp., $19.99; ages 13 and up), a book so engaging and lively it might take you a moment to pinpoint the disquiet you feel upon reaching its end.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/books/review/21-BKS-SUMMER_KIDS/21-BKS-SUMMER_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/21/books/review/21-BKS-SUMMER_KIDS/21-BKS-SUMMER_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20