inal': 'By Karen Weise and David McCabe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCabe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e2f4a29-a0f6-562f-bb30-f0862d108bdb', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e2f4a29-a0f6-562f-bb30-f0862d108bdb'}, {'abstract': 'Hundreds are staging a tribute to Felix Gonzalez-Torres’s edible sculpture. It addresses the grief of today’s pandemic — just as it did the AIDS crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/12/arts/design/fortune-cookies-sculptures.html', 'snippet': 'Hundreds are staging a tribute to Felix Gonzalez-Torres’s edible sculpture. It addresses the grief of today’s pandemic — just as it did the AIDS crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a quiet homecoming: a mound of fortune cookies on a Havana rooftop overlooking a wide, green river. Art lovers come in ones and twos, cracking open a cookie to read their fortunes, sometimes popping the treat in their mouths.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/arts/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/15/arts/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/arts/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3/14VIRUS-FORTUNECOOKIE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '