b_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Seymour', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seymour', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9487883-9137-5d39-b93c-df1f04aecd8b', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9487883-9137-5d39-b93c-df1f04aecd8b'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on thinking about race when choosing a sperm donor and whether it’s OK to call the cops on your neighbors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/magazine/how-should-i-think-about-race-when-considering-a-sperm-donor.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on thinking about race when choosing a sperm donor and whether it’s OK to call the cops on your neighbors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am an American woman, of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry, and I strive to live my life as an active agent against racism and white supremacy. I am beginning to consider having children and am open to bearing a child as a single mother. It is possible to sort through sperm donors by race, eye color, education level and so on. If I choose a donor of color, am I condemning my child to be born into a system designed not to serve them? Or can I use my white privilege to help them fight that system? Would my future child of color feel separated from their heritage with me as their mother? If I choose a white donor, am I succumbing to racist ideas of what traits are “desirable,” or taking the “easy road” in knowing my child will look more like me? What do you think? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Ethicist/21Ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Should I Think About Race When Considering a Sperm Donor?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I’m Considering a Sperm Donor. How Should I Choose the Race of My Child?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black Lives Matter Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'George Floyd Protests (2020)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARTIFICIAL INSEMINATION', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a630b863-4b98-5330-8c1f-8216833f54f2', 'word_count': 1440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a630b863-4b98-5330-8c1f-8216833f54f2'}, {'abstract': 'A world of life and color surrounded me during decades of wandering the woods, without my ever having noticed it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/magazine/the-unexpected-beauty-of-dog-vomit-and-other-slime.html', 'snippet': 'A world of life and color surrounded me during decades of wandering the woods, without my ever having noticed it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They say everybody needs a hobby, and for the past six months, mine has been taking videos and photographs of slime molds in the oak and redwood forests near my home in California. Perhaps we’ve met somewhere on the trail? I was the guy on the ground with a lens clipped to his iPhone, the patient wife, the children asking worriedly from a distance whether a plant was poison oak. Perhaps you may have recognized my “slime-molding outfit”: the frayed Y2K jacket and Old Navy cargo pants caked with mud of many vintages. Perhaps, if you dared ask what I was doing, I popped up and (in those days when strangers stood close to one another) showed you my pictures.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-articleLarge.png', 'height': 441, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 441}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-jumbo.png', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1161, 'width': 1581, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-LOR-01/21Mag-LOR-01-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unexpected Beauty of ‘Dog Vomit’ and Other Slime Molds', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Slime-Mold Sundays', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slime Mold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iNaturalist (Mobile App)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Mason', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mason', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6d0dad1-d36b-5e0b-ba8c-a92b5b00753f', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6d0dad1-d36b-5e0b-ba8c-a92b5b00753f'}, {'abstract': 'A new Superman comic, written by Gene Luen Yang, and a medical memoir about a rare and debilitating disease are both featured in the latest Graphic Content column.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/books/review/superman-smashes-klan-gene-lueng-yang.html', 'snippet': 'A new Superman comic, written by Gene Luen Yang, and a medical memoir about a rare and debilitating disease are both featured in the latest Graphic Content column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are some good stories behind the new DC title SUPERMAN SMASHES THE KLAN (DC Graphic Novels for Young Adults, paper, $16.99). One is the story of the writer. Gene Luen Yang’s parents are immigrants to the United States from Taiwan and China and he grew up in California a comics fan. (His first comic book was a Superman title.) At Berkeley he majored in computer science, and ended up working in engineering before teaching high school for 17 years. Yang pursued comics on the side, self-publishing under the imprint Humble Comics, and eventually crafted a full-length graphic novel: 2006’s dark, edgy “American Born Chinese” — the book that would make his name. He quit his teaching job only in 2016, the same year he became one of a handful of cartoonists ever to win a MacArthur fellowship, and the same year he was appointed a National Ambassador for Young People’s Literature by the Library of Congress.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 505}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1724, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/books/review/28GraphicContent-CHUTE/28GraphicContent-CHUTE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Superman Returns, to Beat Up the Klan', 'kicker': 'Graphic Content', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Superman (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC Comics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yang, Gene Luen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Superman Smashes the Klan (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dancing After TEN: A Graphic Memoir (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gurihiru (Illustrator)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chong, Vivian (Artist)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Webber, Georgia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillary Chute', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chute', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe0258bd-2749-5e92-a03e-f8a4f6bfec9b', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe0258bd-2749-5e92-a03e-f8a4f6bfec9b'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. health care prices are unregulated, opaque and unpredictable. When Congress required insurers to cover Covid-19 testing, a few providers decided to take advantage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/upshot/coronavirus-test-cost-varies-widely.html', 'snippet': 'U.S. health care prices are unregulated, opaque and unpredictable. When Congress required insurers to cover Covid-19 testing, a few providers decided to take advantage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a one-story brick building in suburban Dallas, between a dentist office and a family medicine clinic, is a medical laboratory that has run some of the most expensive coronavirus tests in America.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/merlin_173468862_4704c05b-84db-496b-9e8c-8420933e21b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/merlin_173468862_4704c05b-84db-496b-9e8c-8420933e21b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/merlin_173468862_4704c05b-84db-496b-9e8c-8420933e21b5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/merlin_173468862_4704c05b-84db-496b-9e8c-8420933e21b5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/up-coronavirus-testcosts2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/up-coronavirus-testcosts2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/15/upshot/up-coronavirus-testcosts2/up-coronavirus-testcosts2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Most Coronavirus Tests Cost About $100. Why Did One Cost $2,315?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Charges For a Virus Test Soared to $2,315', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laboratories and Scientific Equipment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/389d0d8d-0e84-553b-b03a-fdbe4aef8782', 'word_count': 1475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/389d0d8d-0e84-553b-b03a-fdbe4aef8782'}, {'abstract': 'How to combat Covid-19 in a developing economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/opinion/poor-countries-coronavirus.html', 'snippet': 'How to combat Covid-19 in a developing economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The coronavirus has struck the world’s most powerful economies first, leading countries in Europe, North America and East Asia to carry out an array of strategies to help control its spread. But as the virus picks up speed across the global south, those policies may not be applicable there. Low- and middle-income countries like Bangladesh, Pakistan, Indonesia and Nigeria should not blindly follow strategies that are sensible for richer nations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/merlin_172737537_4f9f2c0b-9449-4753-8bfa-1f4a38b1c2a4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/merlin_172737537_4f9f2c0b-9449-4753-8bfa-1f4a38b1c2a4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/merlin_172737537_4f9f2c0b-9449-4753-8bfa-1f4a38b1c2a4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/merlin_172737537_4f9f2c0b-9449-4753-8bfa-1f4a38b1c2a4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/16MeriggiMobarak-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/16MeriggiMobarak-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/opinion/16MeriggiMobarak/16MeriggiMobarak-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Country Fought Ebola. It May Beat Another Disease.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Model For Beating Covid-19', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Leone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangladesh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-16T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niccoló F. Meriggi and Ahmed Mushfiq Mobarak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niccoló', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Meriggi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ahmed', 'middlename': 'Mushfiq', 'lastname': 'Mobarak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e84a943-e8ac-533f-82a3-4ad67527cd81', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e84a943-e8ac-533f-82a3-4ad67527cd81'}, {'abstract': 'A large share of strapped school budgets is going to “merit aid” for wealthy kids, as part of a bidding war to enroll high-income students.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/16/opinion/coronavirus-college-rich-kids.html', 'snippet': 'A large share of strapped school budgets is going to “merit aid” for wealthy kids, as part of a bidding war to enroll high-income students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent decades, many institutions of higher education have increasingly been awarding money to students who do not need that aid to afford college. More than half of the 339 public universities sampled in a paper published by New America at least doubled the amount they spent on so-called merit aid from 2001 to 2017; more than 25 percent quadrupled the amount. About two out of every five dollars these schools provided in institutional aid went to students the government deemed able to afford college without need-based aid. The schools do it because well-to-do families, overall, bring the institutions more tuition dollars than their lower income peers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/merlin_156948408_d647c11e-441e-488f-8c85-0b4171a8e6b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/merlin_156948408_d647c11e-441e-488f-8c85-0b4171a8e6b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/merlin_156948408_d647c11e-441e-488f-8c85-0b4171a8e6b5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/merlin_156948408_d647c11e-441e-488f-8c85-0b4171a8e6b5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/16studentloans3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/16/business/16studentloans3/16studentloans3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 