, 'section_name': 'Parenting', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Sohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d34f0912-af3e-5bd4-979e-1588e53bdd1c', 'word_count': 1497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d34f0912-af3e-5bd4-979e-1588e53bdd1c'}, {'abstract': 'A poem that stares straight into grief’s paralysis and the continual climbing out — simply to do whatever small things follow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/18/magazine/poem-black-snow.html', 'snippet': 'A poem that stares straight into grief’s paralysis and the continual climbing out — simply to do whatever small things follow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a series of exquisitely blunt, elegiac poems for his mother, Carl Adamshick returns from her dying days to a changed world: “new calendar” — everything has altered forever. (Many poems in his recent collection, “Birches,” are called “Black Snow.”) There is, for a long time after, the shock of the new reality and the difficulty applying the fact of the change to each moment that comes next. Have we ever seen a better image than these tweezers? This brave book stares straight into grief’s paralysis and the continual climbing out — simply to do whatever small things follow. Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/magazine/21Mag-Poem-01/21Mag-Poem-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Black Snow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-18T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Adamshick and Naomi Shihab Nye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adamshick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Naomi', 'middlename': 'Shihab', 'lastname': 'Nye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90650c04-706d-53d7-8f18-3b2060c8ce7e', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90650c04-706d-53d7-8f18-3b2060c8ce7e'}, {'abstract': 'A debate about  choosing ideas that are 