om asked the utility to make. He asked the company to implement a new “leadership structure,” not to replace its chief executive.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: June 19, 2020', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-06-19T03:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1fff139-7c3e-574d-b70c-d6cfffc88ee8', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1fff139-7c3e-574d-b70c-d6cfffc88ee8'}, {'abstract': 'As Nippon Professional Baseball begins its season after a 91-day delay, Zach Neal will be a rare foreign-born starter for the Saitama Seibu Lions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/19/sports/baseball/japan-nippon-league-zach-neal.html', 'snippet': 'As Nippon Professional Baseball begins its season after a 91-day delay, Zach Neal will be a rare foreign-born starter for the Saitama Seibu Lions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A month before he was supposed to begin his second season in Japan’s Nippon Professional Baseball league, Zach Neal, an American pitcher, received what he called the highest honor in his career. The manager of his team, the Saitama Seibu Lions, gathered the players for a clubhouse ceremony in February to announce that Neal would be the Lions’ opening day starter.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/19/sports/00japan-baseball1-print/merlin_173616972_bffd32b9-6b04-4d29-8f3d-4dc2a95b5916-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06