cocheted across the internet, upending for another generation New York City’s narrative as a bastion of tolerance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/21/nyregion/racist-video-rosedale-queens.html', 'snippet': 'A snippet of the documentary “Rosedale: The Way It Is” has ricocheted across the internet, upending for another generation New York City’s narrative as a bastion of tolerance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The video rolls on a sunny suburban street, and a group of black children bike toward what looks to them like a parade — there’s a small crowd, and an American flag. Suddenly, they’re swarmed by a group of white children, who hurl racial epithets and rocks. Adults gathered nearby do nothing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Racist Attack on Children Was Taped in 1975. We Found Them.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Racist Attack Was Caught on Camera. Nearly 45 Years Later, It Still Stings.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rosedale (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rosedale: The Way It Is (TV Program)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moyers, Bill', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Seventies', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-21T21:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dae5622-eedb-51f7-b019-e9a705e1e411', 'word_count': 1386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dae5622-eedb-51f7-b019-e9a705e1e411'}, {'abstract': 'The attorney general undermined the rule of law by forcing out Geoffrey Berman, the United States attorney in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/21/opinion/trump-bill-barr-geoffrey-berman.html', 'snippet': 'The attorney general undermined the rule of law by forcing out Geoffrey Berman, the United States attorney in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Trump has long made clear that, for him, “rule of law” is a limited-utility slogan. By word and deed, he has demonstrated his belief that the law exists to serve him, personally and politically.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/opinion/21bharara/21bharara-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/21/opinion/21bharara/21bharara-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/21/opinion/21bharara/21bharara-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'