s-pets.html', 'snippet': 'The pandemic’s human toll in New York has been well documented. But what about the dogs and cats of those who become seriously ill?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a trained disaster responder, Dr. Robin Brennen was well versed in proper safety procedures when she entered a coronavirus patient’s apartment on Manhattan’s Upper West Side in late March. She pulled on protective plastic bootees, a face mask and an eye shield.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/16/nyregion/00nycovid-pets1/00nycovid-pets1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pets Left Behind by Covid-19', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caring for the Pets Left Behind in New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Animal Care Centers of NYC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-23T09:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02ecba6c-4039-5d97-8d16-f34407820cfa', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02ecba6c-4039-5d97-8d16-f34407820cfa'}, {'abstract': 'The invisibility of Drake’s Cross in a San Francisco park may make it the most fitting monument to white supremacy in the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/23/opinion/drakes-cross-white-supremacy.html', 'snippet': 'The invisibility of Drake’s Cross in a San Francisco park may make it the most fitting monument to white supremacy in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monuments are symbols, and in times of trouble they prompt symbolic action. They went up as political statements, and they come down as political statements. But it is far easier to topple a monument or change a name than to eradicate racism or counter its long legacy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'mul