20/06/23/arts/music/olivia-records-lesbian-playlist.html', 'snippet': 'The record company was formed in 1973 with the goals of telling queer women’s stories and putting profits in the pockets of gay artists working outside the mainstream music industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1973, five feminists christened their new business: a lesbian record label that would broadcast the nascent liberation movement’s rallying cry. The women who formed Olivia Records, named after the heroine of a Sapphic novel by Dorothy Bussy, had $4,000 to put out a mail-order single that came with a letter requesting donations. It brought them $12,000, which was used to produce “I Know You Know” by Meg Christian, the first of the label’s more than 60 single and LP releases over the next 20 years. Its music provided a soundtrack for watershed moments including the passing of Title IX, Roe v. Wade, and the tennis champion Billie Jean King’s defeat of the self-proclaimed male chauvinist Bobby Riggs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/27/arts/23playlist-olivia/23playlist-olivia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/27/arts/23playlist-olivia/23playlist-olivia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/27/arts/23playlist-olivia/23playlist-olivia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/27/arts/23playlist-olivia/23playlist-olivia-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'cred