ook Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Lombardo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lombardo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/696f3e1d-9d79-55bb-a2f9-c45748c1d005', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/696f3e1d-9d79-55bb-a2f9-c45748c1d005'}, {'abstract': 'Authors aren’t allowed mutual reviews in the Book Review anymore, but in the 1950s there was a moment of kismet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/26/books/when-james-baldwin-and-langston-hughes-reviewed-each-other.html', 'snippet': 'Authors aren’t allowed mutual reviews in the Book Review anymore, but in the 1950s there was a moment of kismet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Book Review, we occasionally delve into our rich archive and discover forgotten gems. In this case, while looking for reviews by James Baldwin, we came across one by Langston Hughes — and then the unusual circumstance that they reviewed each other. In our Feb. 26, 1956 issue Hughes considered Baldwin’s “Notes of a Native Son” and on March 29, 1959  Baldwin returned the volley with a review of Hughes’s “Selected Poems of Langston Hughes.” The writers were mutually harsh but also perceptive about and alive to each other’s creative genius. To read the full text of the reviews, subscribers can visit Times Machine at timesmachine.nytimes.com ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/25/books/review/BaldwinHughesSub/BaldwinHughesSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 569, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/25/books/review/BaldwinHughesSub/BaldwinHughesSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 569}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 