Bidding The Segway Goodbye', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Segway Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kamen, Dean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-06-26T15:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Will Leitch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Will', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leitch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/389f678f-2e42-59b8-8e50-b6042b8b4734', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/389f678f-2e42-59b8-8e50-b6042b8b4734'}, {'abstract': '“The fact I am here means I am descended from people who, even enslaved, did not give up hope.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/26/opinion/let-freedom-ring-from-georgia.html', 'snippet': '“The fact I am here means I am descended from people who, even enslaved, did not give up hope.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — This, an old saying goes, is “the city too busy to hate,” one of the few places in America where enlightened leaders, black and white, chose prosperity over prejudice and a large black middle class emerged decades ago. Birthplace of Martin Luther King Jr., cradle of the civil rights movement, Atlanta, with its gleaming towers and porch swings, was an American exception. The city managed racial conflict through compromise. It was the black mecca. Or so the story went.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/28/opinion/26cohen1a/merlin_173570394_aa1fdc57-f92b-4914-8f57-1aabc131e0f3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 